Sky News has seen the first  comprehensive analysis of patients  needing critical care the cause of  coronavirus and it shows why hospitals  are under such pressure the intensive  care national audit and Research Center  looked at 196 patients admitted to  intensive care in England Wales and  Northern Ireland the numbers steadily  increasing since the start of the month  the numbers are still relatively small  but there are some interesting trends  139 of them were male about 71% their  average age was 62 not as old as you  would expect in fact 10% were in their  40s 5% were in their 30s but it can  affect people who are even younger and  were perfectly healthy I had zero  symptoms I arrived home in the 16th of  March on Monday and by Tuesday I had  horrific shortness of breath and an  extremely heart temperature by Wednesday  it had exacerbated really struggling to  breathe insanely insanely high  temperature convulsions in bed due to my  body fighting the infection on Thursday  I phone an ambulance and I've and I've  been in hospital now for this is the  fifth day three quarters of the patients  needed mechanical ventilation to help  with their breathing in the first 24  hours of being admitted underlining the  importance of sourcing enough  ventilators in the coming weeks so how  did they do well 163 of the patients are  still in critical care 16 have died but  17 have recovered and have been  discharged there's more comprehensive  data on those 33 patients because dead  or alive they have technically completed  their treatment half of them spent nine  days in intensive care  underlining the pressure on beds and it  wasn't just help with their breathing  that they needed 79 percent required  basic cardiovascular support that could  include drug treatment or the insertion  of tubes into their blood vessels 18  percent needed more advanced cardiac  care such as a temporary pacemaker and  12 percent needed kidney dialysis these  are very  seriously ill patients needing expert  care and as the epidemic gets worse more  staff will need to be retrained to give  life-saving care to a growing number of  people
 

